{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# `rebar review` / `review_ticket` looks vestigial — decide whether it should exist\n\n## Why this is being asked\n\nThe operator's read: if a command reviews a ticket in a SINGLE pass, it is likely vestigial, because\nall plan reviews should go through the plan-review process. This ticket gathers the evidence and\nforces an explicit decision rather than leaving a lightly-used LLM entry point in place by inertia.\n\n## Evidence that it is vestigial\n\n**No internal consumer.** `review_ticket` (`llm/operations.py`) is called by nothing inside `src/`\nexcept its own surfaces: the CLI `review` intercept, an MCP tool (`_mcp_llm.py:46`), and the library\nre-export in `llm/__init__.py`. No gate, workflow or op uses it.\n\n**Its MCP surface is off by default.** `_mcp_llm.py:70` refuses it unless the billable-LLM-tools flag\nis set: \"review_ticket is disabled: it makes a live, billable LLM call.\"\n\n**Not used in CI.** No workflow in `.github/workflows/` invokes `rebar review`.\n\n**Squeezed from BELOW by free deterministic checks.** The CLI already ships `clarity-check` (scores a\nticket's clarity), `check-ac` (asserts an Acceptance Criteria block) and `quality-check` (dispatch\nreadiness) — all LLM-free, all exit-code-clean for scripting. Its prompt (`ticket_quality.md`) asks\nfor exactly this ground: \"a clear problem statement and intended outcome\", \"an explicit, checkable\nAcceptance Criteria list\", \"appropriate scope\", \"sound dependencies/ordering\".\n\n**Squeezed from ABOVE by the plan-review gate.** `review-plan` is the three-pass gate that actually\ngates `claim`: ~30 criteria, a blocking DET floor, Pass-2 verification, Pass-4 coaching, a signed\nattestation and a durable sidecar. Its criteria already cover clarity, ACs, scope and dependency\nsoundness. Anything `review` reports, the gate reports with grounding and a signature.\n\n**Stale.** `ticket_quality.md`'s last touch was 2026-06-27, and that commit was an unrelated sweep\n(container fan-out optimization) rather than a change to the reviewer.\n\n## What WOULD be lost — state this before deciding\n\n- a CHEAP, UNSIGNED LLM opinion on a ticket, with no DET floor to satisfy and no attestation written.\n  `review-plan` on a ticket that is not claimable fast-fails by design, and it costs a full gate run;\n  there is currently no \"just give me a quick read on this ticket\" path that uses a model.\n- the `--reviewer` / `--graph` surface, which lets a caller pick a reviewer id or review a ticket\n  graph as a unit. Whether anything depends on that should be checked, not assumed.\n- a library entry point (`rebar.llm.review_ticket`) that external code may import. This is a public\n  surface, so removal is a breaking change for library consumers.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n**DECIDED by the operator: retire `rebar review` and make the `review` verb an ALIAS for the\nplan-review verb.** The evidence above stands as the justification; the criteria below are about\nexecuting it without breaking callers.\n\n### The alias is not flag-compatible — this is the main design problem\n\n    rebar review       [--graph] [--output] [--check] [--ref] [--source]  [ticket_id] [reviewer_id]\n    rebar review-plan            [--output] [--check] [--ref] [--source] [--no-sign] [--force] [--status]  [ticket_id]\n\nSo `--graph` and the positional `reviewer_id` have NO equivalent on the target verb, and\n`review-plan` adds three flags `review` never had. A silent alias would accept `--graph` and ignore\nit, or accept a reviewer id and discard it.\n\n- [ ] `rebar review <ticket>` runs the plan-review gate\n- [ ] `--graph` and a positional `reviewer_id` FAIL LOUDLY with a message naming the replacement,\n      rather than being silently accepted and ignored. Silently dropping an argument that changed the\n      meaning of the command is the same defect class as ticket 00da\n- [ ] the behaviour differences a caller will notice are documented at the alias, because they are\n      not cosmetic: plan-review SIGNS an attestation, enforces a blocking DET floor, FAST-FAILS with\n      no LLM on a ticket that is not claimable, and costs a full multi-pass gate run. Anyone\n      scripting `rebar review` for a cheap unsigned opinion gets none of those properties\n- [ ] the deprecation is announced before removal, following this repo's existing convention\n      (`warn_deprecated`, as used for `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`), so the alias warns for a release rather\n      than changing behaviour silently\n\n### Every surface accounted for\n\n- [ ] CLI `review` intercept -> aliased\n- [ ] MCP tool `review_ticket` (`_mcp_llm.py:46`, already gated behind the billable-tools flag) ->\n      aliased to the `review_plan` tool or removed, decided explicitly\n- [ ] library export `rebar.llm.review_ticket` -> deprecated with a warning, NOT deleted outright:\n      it is a public API and removal is a breaking change for external consumers\n- [ ] `ticket_quality.md` -> removed, or kept with its remaining purpose stated. It is the\n      `default: true` catalog entry, so check `default_reviewer_id()` still resolves for every other\n      consumer before deleting it — that function raises `LLMError` when nothing is marked default\n- [ ] `select_reviewers` / `assemble_context` -> both are documented PUBLIC entry points that other\n      code uses (`assemble_context` is consumed by the completion gate), so they must survive the\n      retirement of the op that motivated them\n- [ ] `docs/llm-framework.md` and any agent guidance listing `review` as an operation are updated\n\n### Do not regress these\n\n- [ ] `rebar clarity-check`, `check-ac` and `quality-check` keep working — they are the free,\n      LLM-less path and part of why this op is redundant\n- [ ] no path is introduced where an unsigned review result can be mistaken for a gate attestation\n\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785639792439187001,"uuid":"6e990991-fca8-4bd0-8dbf-702042d2dc6f"}